ent tempos.\n718\t6\n719\t  By the midpoint of thecontinuous\n720\t          Due to their   second line,construction,\n721\t                                      the melody is moving    eight times\n722\t                                                       these canons    workas just\n723\t                                                                              fast as\n724\t                                                                                   asthe beginning.\n725\t                                                                                       well          Over thetempo\n726\t                                                                                             with irrational  last quarter of the\n727\t                                                                                                                      ratios.  Figure\n728\tmelodic line, every eighth note remains at a constant volume while all other notes fade out, gradually revealing the opening of the\n729\tmelody moving at the same tempo transposed down a minor third.\n730\t    Nancarrow explored irrational tempo ratios in Studies Nos. 40, 11\n731\t7                                                                                     13     2\n732\t                                                                   e : !, and 41, 3 16 :     3.\n733\t\fFigure 10: Canon 4 : 2 : 1.\n734\t\n735\t\n736\t\n737\t\n738\t            12\n739\t\f11 is a three-voice canon in the ratios 𝜋 : 𝑒 : 1, in which theoretically no notes will begin simultaneously.\n740\t(Practically speaking, some notes will begin close enough to be perceived as simultaneous.) In this case,\n741\tthe predominant chord is again the root-position triad. What this means is that the closer two notes are to\n742\tbeginning simultaneously, the more likely they are to form part of a triad. Simultaneously sounding notes in\n743\twhich the attacks are farther apart may form various kinds of incomplete diatonic seventh and ninth chords.\n744\tSince each voice is progressively elaborating the same cycle of ascending fifths in a diatonic manner, the\n745\tvoices will always “agree” as to the prevailing diatonic collection, even as this collection smoothly passes\n746\tthrough all of its twelve transpositions.6\n747\t\n748\t\n749\t5     Table Canons\n750\tAn initially surprising consequence of maximally self-similar lines is that they naturally give rise to canons\n751\tby retrograde inversion, also known as table canons, where the line is combined with itself backward and\n752\tupside down.7 An intuitive understanding of this potential is provided by the geometric properties of the\n753\tlogarithmic spiral, though table canons arise in both the discrete and continuous constructions, as we will\n754\tshortly demonstrate algebraically.\n755\t    Consider Figure 12. If the original melody moves along the logarithmic spiral from the center out in the\n756\tcounterclockwise direction, then the retrograde of the melody will move from the outside toward the center in\n757\tthe clockwise direction. The original melody cannot be combined with its retrograde because the two are\n758\tmoving in opposite directions through the harmonic cycle; e.g., if the original moves through the ascending\n759\tcircle of fifths, then its retrograde will move through the descending circle of fifths, and we will be unable to\n760\tmaintain harmonic consistency.\n761\t    Similarly, the inversion of the original melody will proceed in the opposite direction. We can represent the\n762\tinversion by inverting the spiral about the 𝑥-axis, shown in Figure 13 by the blue spiral, where the inversion of\n763\tthe melody would move from the center out in the clockwise direction. However, as shown in the figure, the\n764\tretrograde inversion moves in the counterclockwise direction and thus moves in the same direction through\n765\tthe harmonic cycle as the original melody; e.g., if the melody moves through an ascending circle of fifths,\n766\tthen its retrograde inversion will also move through the ascending circle of fifths.\n767\t    We proceed with a couple of examples featuring the melody from §4, now in canon by retrograde inversion.\n768\tIn the first example, the two forms of the melody begin and end together in a two-voice table canon. However,\n769\tthe original version continually accelerates, while the retrograde inversion continually decelerates, preserving\n770\tthe harmonic rhythm at a fixed tempo (as discussed in §2.6). Throughout, the predominant interval is the\n771\ttenth.\n772\t    Our final canon is a six-voice table canon which combines a three-voice canon in the ratios 28 : 24 : 21\n773\twith its three-voice retrograde inversion. Within each of the three-voice canons the predominant interval is\n774\tthe octave, while between the retrograde inversions the primary interval is the tenth. Thus this canon sounds\n775\tlike a texturally enriched version of the preceding canon.\n776\t    Algebraically, this self-similarity by retrograde inversion is a direct consequence of our homomorphism\n777\tfor directed intervals, 𝜙. Recall the discussion in §2.6 of the difference between linear and log time, with the\n778\tdifference between two time points defined by their ratio in the latter. A retrograde in log time, what one\n779\tmight call a proportional retrograde, maps time point 𝑟 to time point 𝑡02 /𝑟, where 𝑡0 is the fixed temporal axis\n780\tof the retrograde. For example, if the axis is 𝑡0 = 4 and we have the sequence 𝑟 = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, then the\n781\t   6 For precedents of irrational tempo canons, see Nancarrow’s Studies Nos. 33, 40, and 41. See also Paul Usher’s arrangement of\n782\t               √\n783\tStudy No. 33, 2/2, for the Arditti Quartet, discussed in Callender [5]. For more on Nancarrow’s tempo canons, their structural\n784\tproperties, and tempo dissonance see Gann [7], Nemire [12], and Thomas [13] [14].\n785\t   7 The score for such a canon can be placed on a table and read by two performers on opposite sides, giving rise to the term table\n786\t\n787\tcanon. One example is often attributed to Mozart, Der Spiegel (The Mirror) for two violins.\n788\t\n789\t\n790\t\n791\t                                                                13\n792\t\fFigure 11: Canon 𝜋 : 𝑒 : 1.\n793\t\n794\t\n795\t\n796\t\n797\t            14\n798\t\f                                                                     retrograde\n799\t\n800\t\n801\t\n802\t\n803\tFigure 12: The retrograde of the original theme moves in the opposite direction through the harmonic cycle.\n804\t\n805\t\n806\t\n807\t\n808\t                                                                     retrograde\n809\t                                                                     inversion\n810\t\n811\t\n812\t\n813\t\n814\tFigure 13: The retrograde inversion of the original theme moves in the same direction through the harmonic\n815\tcycle.\n816\t\n817\t\n818\t\n819\t\n820\t                                                    15\n821\t\fFigure 14: Table canon (canon by retrograde inversion).\n822\t\n823\t\n824\t\n825\t\n826\t                          16\n827\t\fFigure 15: Table canon 28 : 24 : 21.\n828\t\n829\t\n830\t                17\n831\t\f                                 𝑡2\n832\tproportional retrograde is 𝑟0 = 16, 8, 16        16 8 16\n833\t                                        3 , 4, 5 , 3 , 7 , 2. The directed intervals for the retrograde are\n834\t                                                  !\n835\t                                              𝑡02\n836\t                                           𝜙        = 2𝜙(𝑡0 ) − 𝜙(𝑟).                                       (9)\n837\t                                              𝑟\n838\tIn other words, the proportional retrograde yields a transposed (2𝜙(𝑡0 )) inversion (−𝜙(𝑟)) of the original line.\n839\t(Note also how this equation relates to musical inversion of a pitch, 𝑝, about an axis, 𝑛: 2𝑛 − 𝑝.) Reflection in\n840\ttime corresponds to a reflection in pitch, and as a consequence, these lines are inversionally symmetric (up to\n841\ttransposition and in log time) about every time point.\n842\t    If we take the inversion of the retrograde, we have\n843\t                                                    !\n844\t                                                𝑡02\n845\t                                            −𝜙        = 𝜙(𝑟) − 2𝜙(𝑡 0 ),                                     (10)\n846\t                                                𝑟\n847\twhich is simply a transposed version of the original—under the conditions of maximal self-similarity,\n848\tretrograde inversion collapses to transposition. As demonstrated by the above examples, melodic realizations\n849\tof this structure remain perceptually distinct under retrograde inversion, provided the line is not based solely\n850\ton a logarithmic series of time points. It is the underlying structural equivalence that makes the complex\n851\tcombinations of these lines possible.\n852\t\n853\t\n854\t6     Conclusion\n855\tWe have constructed maximally self-similar lines in (1) the discrete case, where positive rational time points\n856\tare mapped to real-valued directed intervals through prime factorization and a freely chosen map from the\n857\tprimes to the reals; and (2) the continuous case, where real-valued time points are associated with directed\n858\tintervals through quantized logarithms mapped to harmonic cycles. The main result in both cases is that the\n859\tvertical interval between voices in a tempo ratio of 𝜆𝑖 /𝜆 𝑗 is given by the homomorphism 𝜙(𝜆𝑖 /𝜆 𝑗 ), which is\n860\ta constant independent of time. Furthermore, we demonstrated that under these constructions retrograde\n861\tinversion collapses to transposition. The musical payoff of the mathematics is the ability to compose melodic\n862\tlines that combine in any number of voices, in any tempo ratios, either forward or in retrograde inversion\n863\twhile maintaining harmonic consistency—lines with an infinite number of canonic combinations.\n864\t     Future work will proceed along a number of lines. First, there are many avenues to explore other\n865\tmusical realizations (including a variety of harmonic cycles),8 multimedia presentations, an interactive app,\n866\tand relation to recent work by Tymoczko [16]. Second, it is worth considering the relationships between\n867\tthe current approach and that of Johnson, Amiot, and others in greater detail than §2.7. Third, the pitch\n868\tsymmetry about every time point noted toward the end of §5 suggests an alternative formalization in terms\n869\tof reflection. Fourth, it would be helpful and musically suggestive to consider the move from the discrete\n870\tto the continuous case in greater detail: one can incorporate irrational ratios within the construction of\n871\t§2 through Hamel bases at the expense of potentially wildly discontinuous structures, creating problems\n872\tfor the harmonic consistency that motivated the requirement for continuity in §3.2, while the quantization\n873\tprocess may also present difficulties for maintaining harmonic control. Relatedly, it is important to clearly\n874\tidentify the effects of rounding errors alluded to at the end of §4. Fifth, the function 𝜙 invites discussion of\n875\thomomorphisms between Lewinian generalized interval systems [11] (following Tymoczko’s response [15] to\n876\tHook [9]), particularly the GISes corresponding to products over the positive rationals and addition over the\n877\treals discussed in Lewin [11]. Finally, proof of the expanded aperiodicity results in §2.5 is forthcoming.\n878\t    8 These realizations include Canonic Offerings, for string quartet, invited by Dmitri Tymoczko for a concert as part of the 2013\n879\t\n880\tBridges Conference: Mathematics, Music, Art, Architecture, Culture in Enschede, The Netherlands and spira mirabilis: infinite\n881\ttempo canons, for player piano, invited by Benjamin Broening for 2018 Third Practice Music Festival at the University of Richmond,\n882\tVA.\n883\t\n884\t\n885\t                                                                18\n886\t\fReferences\n887\t[1] Amiot, Emmanuel, “Autosimilar Melodies,” Journal of Mathematics and Music 2, no. 3 (2008): 157–180.\n888\t\n889\t[2] Amiot, Emmanuel, Carlos Agon, and Moreno Andreatta, “Autosimilar Melodies and Their Implementation\n890\t    in OpenMusic,” Proceedings of the 4th Sound and Music Computing Conference, Lefkada, Greece, 2007.\n891\t\n892\t[3] Archibald, R. C., “Notes on the Logarithmic Spiral, Golden Section and the Fibonacci Series,” Note V in\n893\t    Jay Hambidge, Dynamic Symmetry: The Greek Vase, 146–157. New Haven: Yale University Press, 1920.\n894\t\n895\t[4] Callender, Clifton, “Formalized Accelerando: An Extension of Rhythmic Techniques in Nancarrow’s\n896\t    Acceleration Canons,” Perspectives of New Music 39, no. 1 (2001): 188–210.\n897\t\n898\t[5] Callender,\n899\t    √          Clifton, “Performing the Irrational: Paul Usher’s Arrangement of Nancarrow’s Study No. 33,\n900\t     2/2,” Music Theory Online 20, no. 1 (2014).\n901\t\n902\t[6] Callender, Clifton, “Infinite Canons,” https://cliftoncallender.com/research/infinite_\n903\t    canons/.\n904\t\n905\t[7] Gann, Kyle, The Music of Conlon Nancarrow. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1995.\n906\t\n907\t[8] Hendriks, Dimitri, Frits G. W. Dannenberg, Jörg Endrullis, Mark Dow, and Jan Willem Klop, “Arithmetic\n908\t    Self-Similarity of Infinite Sequences,” arXiv:1201.3786 [math], 2012.\n909\t\n910\t[9] Hook, Julian, “Cross-Type Transformations and the Path Consistency Condition,” Music Theory Spectrum\n911\t    29, no. 1 (2007): 1–40.\n912\t\n913\t[10] Johnson, Tom, Self-Similar Melodies. Paris: Editions 75, 1996.\n914\t\n915\t[11] Lewin, David, Generalized Musical Intervals and Transformations. New Haven: Yale University Press,\n916\t    1987.\n917\t\n918\t[12] Nemire, Julie, “Convergence Points in Conlon Nancarrow’s Tempo Canons,” Music Theory Online 20,\n919\t    no. 1 (2014).\n920\t\n921\t[13] Thomas, Margaret E., “Nancarrow’s Canons: Projections of Temporal and Formal Structures,” Perspec-\n922\t    tives of New Music 38, no. 2 (2000): 106–133.\n923\t\n924\t[14] Thomas, Margaret, “Nancarrow’s Canons: Proportions and Processes,” Intégral 14/15 (2000/2001):\n925\t    139–174.\n926\t\n927\t[15] Tymoczko, Dmitri, “Lewin, Intervals, and Transformations: A Comment on Hook,” Music Theory\n928\t    Spectrum 30, no. 1 (2008): 164–168.\n929\t\n930\t[16] Tymoczko, Dmitri, “The American Mathematical Society Presents: The Music of Mathematics,”\n931\t    International Congress of Mathematicians, Philadelphia, July 28, 2026 (guest lecture/performance).\n932\t\n933\t\n934\t\n935\t\n936\t                                                    19\n937\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f1d34183-2613-4a14-bbab-3ac2f80e7ccd", "uuid": "a541a67b-006f-498e-bbd4-7e9fe12e4822", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T10:12:00.773Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/canon_text.txt", "content": "                                         Infinite Canons: Maximally Self-Similar Melodic Lines and Canons\n                                                              with Infinite Solutions\n                                                                                                Clifton Callender∗\n                                                                                              Florida State University\n\n                                                                                                   July 28, 2026\n\n\n                                                                                                       Abstract\n                                                     Infinite Canons is an ongoing series of canons with infinite solutions. More specifically, each canon\n                                                 is based on a melodic line that can be combined in any number of voices, at any tempo ratios (rational\narXiv:2607.23210v1 [cs.SD] 25 Jul 2026\n\n\n\n\n                                                 or irrational), and with each voice moving either forward or in retrograde inversion, while maintaining\n                                                 harmonic consistency. This paper describes the structure of these maximally self-similar melodic lines\n                                                 based on two different constructions: 1) a discrete prime-factorization approach yielding self-similarity\n                                                 under all rational ratios, and 2) a continuous logarithmic approach extending this to irrational ratios. In both\n                                                 cases the vertical interval between voices in a tempo ratio of 𝜆𝑖 /𝜆 𝑗 is given by a homomorphism 𝜙(𝜆𝑖 /𝜆 𝑗 ),\n                                                 which is a constant independent of time. Furthermore, under these constructions retrograde inversion\n                                                 collapses to transposition, allowing for all manner of table canons. These structures are demonstrated\n                                                 with suggestive realizations of several different infinite canons. Future work includes a more complete\n                                                 mathematical treatment, musical applications, and an interactive program that allows users to explore an\n                                                 unlimited number of realizations of these pieces.\n\n\n                                         1     Introduction\n                                         A musical canon is a composition in which a melodic line is placed in counterpoint with itself according to a\n                                         rule combining temporal shift, transposition, inversion, retrograde, and/or augmentation or diminution. Most\n                                         canons have a single solution—a single rule by which the line combines with itself successfully—but some\n                                         canons have more. Figure 1 shows the two non-trivially distinct solutions for the two-voice canon marked\n                                         “Quaerendo invenietis” from J. S. Bach’s Musical Offering. The existence of canons that work under multiple\n                                         rules raises an interesting question: Is there a limit to the number of solutions a canonic line can possess?\n                                              One of Conlon Nancarrow’s more curious works is Study No. 44, which Kyle Gann [7] calls the “Aleatory\n                                         Canon.” The canon is for two voices in any tempo ratio. (Each voice of the canon is played on a different\n                                         piano using a separate roll.) Nancarrow maintains global harmonic control by keeping his materials very\n                                         simple. (The two voices combined mostly stay within a single diatonic collection.) However, local harmonic\n                                         details are essentially unplanned, since the exact alignment of the two canonic voices is unknowable ahead\n                                         of time. This raises another question: Is it possible to create a canon that works in any tempo ratio while\n                                         maintaining control of local-level harmonic details?\n                                             ∗ Earlier versions of this material were presented as “Spira mirabilis, for player piano,” Joint Mathematics Meetings, New Orleans,\n\n                                         January 6, 2011 (invited presentation; abstract 1067-00-1969, Abstracts of Papers Presented to the American Mathematical Society\n                                         32, no. 1); “Infinitely variable tempo canons,” Nancarrow in the 21st Century International Conference, Southbank Centre / Trinity\n                                         Laban Conservatoire of Music and Dance, London, April 21–22, 2012; and “Maximally Self-Similar Melodies and Canons with\n                                         Infinite Solutions,” joint annual meeting of the American Musicological Society, Society for Ethnomusicology, and Society for\n                                         Music Theory, New Orleans, November 1–4, 2012. Related material is posted at https://cliftoncallender.com/research/\n                                         infinite_canons/. An expanded and more developed treatment of these topics is in preparation.\n\n\n                                                                                                           1\n\f2                                                          autosimilar melodies\n\n\n  The present paper is on the one hand a mathematical study of orbits of aﬃne maps operating on a\ncyclic group. On the other hand it is developing a musically interesting notion of autosimilarity, just like\nfamous fractals (the Cantor Set, Koch flake, Sierpinski sponge) but discrete. Diverse musical renderings are\npossible; in the simplest a melody plays within itself contrapunctically, something the Cantor of Leipzig\nmight have dreamed of. Several instances of such melodies have been identified in classical music.\n\n\n1     First definitions, historical examples.\n\nWe begin with the simplest case, when all augmentations begin on the same note. This is historically the\ncase studied by Tom Johnson in [7], though he came across the more general case, with diﬀerent starting\npoints, which\n        Figure will be studied\n               1: Two  solutionsinforsection III; otherinvenietis”\n                                      the “Quaerendo     generalizations  will occur\n                                                                   canon from         in theMusical\n                                                                               J. S. Bach’s  last sections.\n                                                                                                    Offering.\n\n\n1.1     This paper provides\n       Autosimilar  melodyan answer\n                            with     to a.\n                                 ratio  both questions by constructing melodic lines that can be combined in\n    any number of voices, in any tempo ratios (rational or irrational), and with each voice moving either forward,\nDefinition\n    or backward1.1andLet M be\n                      upside   a periodic\n                             down            melody\n                                   through the        with maintaining\n                                               line, while   period n: Mharmonic\n                                                                          0 , . . . Mn consistency.\n                                                                                        = M0 , Mn+1The\n                                                                                                     = key\n                                                                                                       M1 ,is. .that\n                                                                                                                 . wherein\n                                                                                                                     the\nthe canonic\n    values M     are  musical  events  (pitch classes   for  instance)  and    k   is some  measure of time.     M   is au-\n               k must be maximally self similar; that is, the melody must be self similar at all possible time scales.\n            line\ntosimilar  1 with ratio a iﬀ\n        Tom Johnson’s La vie est si courte, shown in Figure 2, is an example of self similarity at a single time\n    scale. The lower line is the same as the upper line played three times as slow. Note that the beginning of\n                                                 ∀k ∈ Zn Ma k = Mk\n    every note in the lower line forms an octave with the   upper line. So the melody of La vie est si courte is self\n    similar at the ratio of 3:1. Equivalently, the melody gives rise to a 3:1 tempo canon that exhibits maximal\n  This means that taking one note every a beats lets hear the same melody, only slower; or equivalently\n    harmonic consistency—a series of parallel octaves. We can enhance the independence of the two lines while\nthat some augmentation of the melody is part of the melody itself, as is quite obvious on the score below\n    obscuring the melody’s self-similar structure by transposing one of the voices. For instance, transposing the\n(with a = 3). This explains why the melody has to be infinite. Non-periodic solutions are possible2 , but\n    upper line of this 3:1 tempo canon up a diatonic third (assuming an implicit key signature of one sharp) yields\nthis would be another subject.\n    a series of parallel tenths, shown in Figure 3. Johnson [10], Emmanuel Amiot [1], and others have written\n    about these self-similar melodies at finite time scales (see §2.7), but we will need to use a different approach\n1.2 to Musical\n       extend this  important idea to all possible time scales, with the closest prior treatment being Hendriks et\n                  examples\n    al. [8], discussed in §2.4.\n\n\n\n\n                                         Figure 1.LaFirst\n                        Figure 2: Tom Johnson’s      vie bars\n                                                          est siof courte\n                                                                   ’la Vie est si courte’\n                                                                           is self  similar at the ratio 3:1.\n\n         This paper details the construction of maximally self-similar lines in two cases. Section 2 constructs such\n    1 We decided to change Tom Johnson’s ‘selfRep’ to ‘autoSimilar’, that he himself used in the broad sense of ‘product of some iterated\nprocess’, because it is really the traditional mathematical meaning, for instance with classical fractals.\n   2 like motif xx enlarged, e.g. . . . dcbaabcd. . . obtained from aa, b..b, c....c and so on.\n                                                                     2\n\f                           #               œ œ œ               œ\n                      &            œ   œ               œ            œ        œ œ œ   œ       œ        œ   œ œ œ        œ\n\n                      ? # ˙.                       ˙           œ    œ.         œ.    œ       ˙            ˙.\n\n\n                    # œ                                                              œ\n                               œ       œ œ œ   œ           œ       œ œ œ       œ         œ       œ œ œ         œ\n            4\n\n                &                                      œ                                                           œ\n                      ˙.                   ˙           œ                œ.     œ     ˙\n                ?#                                         œ.                                    ˙.                œ\n            4\n\n\n\n\nFigure 3: Tom Johnson’s La vie est si courte. Here the self-similarity is manifest as a 3:1 tempo canon where\nsimultaneous attacks form a series of parallel tenths.\n\n\nlines in the discrete case, allowing for infinite combinations at all rational tempo ratios, by freely assigning\ndirected intervals to every prime number and using prime factorization to define the map from time points to\ndirected intervals 𝜙 : (Q+ , ×) −→ (R, +). Intervals are determined entirely by the ratios of time points, which\nis the necessary and sufficient condition for maximal self similarity. Section 3 extends this approach through\nCauchy’s logarithmic functional equation to the continuous case, including irrational ratios. Throughout\nboth Sections 2 and 3, we consider structural properties of these lines, including issues of periodicity and\naperiodicity and the temporal relationship between time points and harmonic rhythm.\n     The musical significance of these structures is demonstrated in Section 4 by two canons based on the\nsame lines with tempo ratios 4 : 2 : 1 and 𝜋 : 𝑒 : 1, respectively. Maximally self-similar lines naturally give rise\nto canons by retrograde inversion, sometimes called table canons, which is the subject of Section 5, revealing\na strong connection between retrograde and inversion. A concluding section points to future elaborations,\nincluding a greater variety of musical realizations, recent work by Dmitri Tymoczko, and more thorough\nengagement with the underlying mathematics, including the work of Hendriks et al. and proof of the extension\nof their aperiodicity results.\n\n\n2     The Discrete Construction\n2.1\nIn this section we detail a discrete approach to constructing melodic lines that are self similar at all positive\nrational time scales. These are melodies that can combine with any rational augmentation or diminution to\ncreate a tempo canon maintaining harmonic consistency. Consider the two-voice four-note canon diagrammed\nin Figure 4, where the second voice is an augmentation of the first by a factor of 𝜆. This means that every\ntime point, 𝑡, in the second voice coincides with the first at 𝜆𝑡. In particular, time points 1 and 𝑞 in the second\nvoice coincide with time points 𝜆 and 𝜆𝑞 in the first. We indicate the pitch interval from time point 1 to 𝜆 in\nthe first voice by 𝜙(𝜆), using the ratio of time points, 𝜆/1 = 𝜆. In order for time point 1 in the second voice to\nform a unison with the first, the entirety of the augmented voice must be transposed by this same interval,\n𝜙(𝜆). Note also that the interval from 1 to 𝜆𝑞 in voice one (and to 𝑞 in voice two), 𝜙(𝜆𝑞), is equal to the\nsum of the interval for each factor: 𝜙(𝜆𝑞) = 𝜙(𝜆) + 𝜙(𝑞). This is the essential homomorphism from the\nmultiplicative realm of positive, rational time points and the additive realm of real-valued intervals, and the\n\n\n\n                                                                    3\n\fcrux of our construction:\n                                                         𝜙 : (Q+ , ×) −→ (R, +).                                                          (1)\nSince each rational time point, 𝑞 ∈ Q+ , has a unique prime factorization, we can fully determine a maximally\nself-similar melody by defining a real-valued interval for each prime number.1\n                                                                                          ( q) = ( ) + (q)\n\n                                                                                                                 (q)\n                                                                          ()                      (q/ )\n                                voice 2\n                            time points              1                                                       q              q\n                                                                                   (q)                                 ()\n\n\n                                  ()                           ( q)\n\n                                                                                   (q)\n                                          ()                      (q/ )\n    voice 1\ntime points             1                                                   q            ()                  q\n                                               (q)\n\n                                                         ( q) = ( ) + (q)\n\n\nFigure 4: Diagram of a two-voice four-note canon by augmentation at the rational factor 𝜆. In order for\nsimultaneous time points to form a unison (and thus ensure self-similarity), the second voice (green) must be\na transposition of the first (blue) by the interval 𝜙(𝜆). Intervals relate to time points and combine according\nto the homomorphism 𝜙(𝜆𝑞) = 𝜙(𝜆) + 𝜙(𝑞).\n\n\n2.2\n                                                                                                                                Î\nLet 𝜏( 𝑝) ∈ R be the freely chosen directed interval associated with prime 𝑝. For any time point 𝑞 =                                𝑝 𝑝\n                                                                                                                                          𝑎𝑝 ,\n\n𝑎 𝑝 ∈ Z, the interval from time point 1 to 𝑞 is\n                                                      ∑︁\n                                             𝜙(𝑞) =       𝑎 𝑝 𝜏( 𝑝).                                                                      (2)\n                                                                               𝑝\n\n(In particular 𝜙( 𝑝) = 𝜏( 𝑝) on primes.) Assuming a reference pitch 𝑠 at time point 1, where 𝜙(1) = 0, the\npitch at time point 𝑞 is\n                                                𝑇𝜙 (𝑞) (𝑠).                                             (3)\n    This completes the construction of a maximally self-similar melodic line. As discussed in §2.1, to\nmaintain harmonic unisons at coincident time points in augmentation canon by 𝜆, the augmented voice must\nbe transposed by 𝑇𝜙 (𝜆) . More generally, for an 𝑛-voice tempo canon with voices in the ratios 𝜆1 , 𝜆2 , . . . , 𝜆 𝑛 ,\nwhere 𝜆𝑖 is the particular ratio for voice 𝑖, the interval from voice 𝑖 to voice 𝑗 at a global time point 𝑡 is given\nby                                           \u0012 \u0013        \u0012 \u0013       \u0012 \u0013\n                                                𝑡        𝑡          𝜆𝑗\n                                           𝜙        −𝜙        = 𝜙        .                                        (4)\n                                               𝜆𝑗        𝜆𝑖         𝜆𝑖\nThat is, the harmonic interval between voices is a constant independent of time, which is precisely the lever for\nexerting harmonic control between voices in all possible rational tempo ratios. Since the harmonic interval is\na constant, we can transpose the voices relative to one another to achieve the desired harmonic combinations.\n    1 Hendriks et al. [8] study one-sided infinite sequences over a finite alphabet. Restricted to integer time points and to pitch classes\n\nrather than pitches, our sequences are identical to theirs. See §2.4 for more on the relationship with their work.\n\n\n                                                                               4\n\f2.3\nThe melody in Example 5 serves to demonstrate the formalism through a tangible musical application. The\nmelody is stated in full in the top staff (harpsichord) and is based on the following mappings of primes\nto directed intervals (measured in semitones): 𝜏(2) = 4, 𝜏(3) = 9, 𝜏(5) = 11, 𝜏(7) = 14, 𝜏(11) = 4,\n𝜏(13) = 10, and 𝜏(17) = 9. This mapping combined with the reference pitch of 𝐶4 at time point 1 fully\ndetermines the pitches for every time point in the melody, as shown in Table 1. Note that the violin, marimba,\n\n                                              Time point              Factors          Interval from time point 1\n                                                  1             20 30 50 · · ·         0\n                                                  2                  21                4\n                                                  3                  31                9\n                                                  4                  22            