                                                                                                                     the same\nwhile  the background\n    tempo    transposed downharmonic\n                                   a minorcycle   varied\n                                              third. The with    respect\n                                                           portion  of thetoline\n                                                                              time.  In thisthe\n                                                                                 between      example,    it is the\n                                                                                                repeat signs    can background\n                                                                                                                     thus repeat ad\nthatinfinitum,\n     moves atdown a fixed  rate, while\n                         a minor           the tempo\n                                   third with           varies. Though the underlying structure is the same, the\n                                                each repetition.\nmelodies seem to have very little in common.6\n\n\n\n\nFigure 8. Melody resulting from the process in Figure 7.\n     Figure 9: Canon line resulting from time points six through twelve and the diminution process shown in\n     Figure 8.\n      Figure 9 demonstrates the potential for maximally self-similar melodies to maintain harmonic\nconsistency in even more complicated combinations. Figure 9a is a tempo canon in irrational ratios of ! :\n       7 We begin with a relatively straightforward three-voice canon in the ratios 4 : 2 : 1 in Figure 10, where notes\ne : 1.starting\n         The musical    effect of\n               simultaneously      irrational\n                                always    formtempo       ratios is triad.\n                                                 a root-position    that theIn notes   of each\n                                                                               this case         voicevoice\n                                                                                           the fastest  will never\n                                                                                                              beginsbegin\n                                                                                                                       alone. Since\nsimultaneously.     Nevertheless,     nearly    simultaneous     attacks    form   a  series of  parallel major\n     the canon line is accelerating, the second voice enters once the first voice has accelerated by a factor      triads,  a of two,\nconsonant     chordhalfway\n    which occurs     that enhances\n                             throughthe the rhythmic\n                                             first line ofdissonance.\n                                                           the melody Figure       9b is a canon\n                                                                        above. Similarly,           in which\n                                                                                              the third        the upper\n                                                                                                        voice will           voicethe\n                                                                                                                     enter when     is\nthe same as the lower voice moving backward and upside down, what musicians term the retrograde\n     first voice has accelerated by a factor of four, which occurs at the end of the first line of the melody. Since the\n     acceleration of each voice is exponential, the time delay between voices remains constant even though the\n     voices are in different tempos.\n6\n  By the midpoint of thecontinuous\n          Due to their   second line,construction,\n                                      the melody is moving    eight times\n                                                       these canons    workas just\n                                                                              fast as\n                                                                                   asthe beginning.\n                                                                                       well          Over thetempo\n                                                                                             with irrational  last quarter of the\n                                                                                                                      ratios.  Figure\nmelodic line, every eighth note remains at a constant volume while all other notes fade out, gradually revealing the opening of the\nmelody moving at the same tempo transposed down a minor third.\n    Nancarrow explored irrational tempo ratios in Studies Nos. 40, 11\n7                                                                                     13     2\n                                                                   e : !, and 41, 3 16 :     3.\n\fFigure 10: Canon 4 : 2 : 1.\n\n\n\n\n            12\n\f11 is a three-voice canon in the ratios 𝜋 : 𝑒 : 1, in which theoretically no notes will begin simultaneously.\n(Practically speaking, some notes will begin close enough to be perceived as simultaneous.) In this case,\nthe predominant chord is again the root-position triad. What this means is that the closer two notes are to\nbeginning simultaneously, the more likely they are to form part of a triad. Simultaneously sounding notes in\nwhich the attacks are farther apart may form various kinds of incomplete diatonic seventh and ninth chords.\nSince each voice is progressively elaborating the same cycle of ascending fifths in a diatonic manner, the\nvoices will always “agree” as to the prevailing diatonic collection, even as this collection smoothly passes\nthrough all of its twelve transpositions.6\n\n\n5     Table Canons\nAn initially surprising consequence of maximally self-similar lines is that they naturally give rise to canons\nby retrograde inversion, also known as table canons, where the line is combined with itself backward and\nupside down.7 An intuitive understanding of this potential is provided by the geometric properties of the\nlogarithmic spiral, though table canons arise in both the discrete and continuous constructions, as we will\nshortly demonstrate algebraically.\n    Consider Figure 12. If the original melody moves along the logarithmic spiral from the center out in the\ncounterclockwise direction, then the retrograde of the melody will move from the outside toward the center in\nthe clockwise direction. The original melody cannot be combined with its retrograde because the two are\nmoving in opposite directions through the harmonic cycle; e.g., if the original moves through the ascending\ncircle of fifths, then its retrograde will move through the descending circle of fifths, and we will be unable to\nmaintain harmonic consistency.\n    Similarly, the inversion of the original melody will proceed in the opposite direction. We can represent the\ninversion by inverting the spiral about the 𝑥-axis, shown in Figure 13 by the blue spiral, where the inversion of\nthe melody would move from the center out in the clockwise direction. However, as shown in the figure, the\nretrograde inversion moves in the counterclockwise direction and thus moves in the same direction through\nthe harmonic cycle as the original melody; e.g., if the melody moves through an ascending circle of fifths,\nthen its retrograde inversion will also move through the ascending circle of fifths.\n    We proceed with a couple of examples featuring the melody from §4, now in canon by retrograde inversion.\nIn the first example, the two forms of the melody begin and end together in a two-voice table canon. However,\nthe original version continually accelerates, while the retrograde inversion continually decelerates, preserving\nthe harmonic rhythm at a fixed tempo (as discussed in §2.6). Throughout, the predominant interval is the\ntenth.\n    Our final canon is a six-voice table canon which combines a three-voice canon in the ratios 28 : 24 : 21\nwith its three-voice retrograde inversion. Within each of the three-voice canons the predominant interval is\nthe octave, while between the retrograde inversions the primary interval is the tenth. Thus this canon sounds\nlike a texturally enriched version of the preceding canon.\n    Algebraically, this self-similarity by retrograde inversion is a direct consequence of our homomorphism\nfor directed intervals, 𝜙. Recall the discussion in §2.6 of the difference between linear and log time, with the\ndifference between two time points defined by their ratio in the latter. A retrograde in log time, what one\nmight call a proportional retrograde, maps time point 𝑟 to time point 𝑡02 /𝑟, where 𝑡0 is the fixed temporal axis\nof the retrograde. For example, if the axis is 𝑡0 = 4 and we have the sequence 𝑟 = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, then the\n   6 For precedents of irrational tempo canons, see Nancarrow’s Studies Nos. 33, 40, and 41. See also Paul Usher’s arrangement of\n               √\nStudy No. 33, 2/2, for the Arditti Quartet, discussed in Callender [5]. For more on Nancarrow’s tempo canons, their structural\nproperties, and tempo dissonance see Gann [7], Nemire [12], and Thomas [13] [14].\n   7 The score for such a canon can be placed on a table and read by two performers on opposite sides, giving rise to the term table\n\ncanon. One example is often attributed to Mozart, Der Spiegel (The Mirror) for two violins.\n\n\n\n                                                                13\n\fFigure 11: Canon 𝜋 : 𝑒 : 1.\n\n\n\n\n            14\n\f                                                                     retrograde\n\n\n\n\nFigure 12: The retrograde of the original theme moves in the opposite direction through the harmonic cycle.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                     retrograde\n                                                                     inversion\n\n\n\n\nFigure 13: The retrograde inversion of the original theme moves in the same direction through the harmonic\ncycle.\n\n\n\n\n                                                    15\n\fFigure 14: Table canon (canon by retrograde inversion).\n\n\n\n\n                          16\n\fFigure 15: Table canon 28 : 24 : 21.\n\n\n                17\n\f                                 𝑡2\nproportional retrograde is 𝑟0 = 16, 8, 16        16 8 16\n                                        3 , 4, 5 , 3 , 7 , 2. The directed intervals for the retrograde are\n                                                  !\n                                              𝑡02\n                                           𝜙        = 2𝜙(𝑡0 ) − 𝜙(𝑟).                                       (9)\n                                              𝑟\nIn other words, the proportional retrograde yields a transposed (2𝜙(𝑡0 )) inversion (−𝜙(𝑟)) of the original line.\n(Note also how this equation relates to musical inversion of a pitch, 𝑝, about an axis, 𝑛: 2𝑛 − 𝑝.) Reflection in\ntime corresponds to a reflection in pitch, and as a consequence, these lines are inversionally symmetric (up to\ntransposition and in log time) about every time point.\n    If we take the inversion of the retrograde, we have\n                                                    !\n                                                𝑡02\n                                            −𝜙        = 𝜙(𝑟) − 2𝜙(𝑡 0 ),                                     (10)\n                                                𝑟\nwhich is simply a transposed version of the original—under the conditions of maximal self-similarity,\nretrograde inversion collapses to transposition. As demonstrated by the above examples, melodic realizations\nof this structure remain perceptually distinct under retrograde inversion, provided the line is not based solely\non a logarithmic series of time points. It is the underlying structural equivalence that makes the complex\ncombinations of these lines possible.\n\n\n6     Conclusion\nWe have constructed maximally self-similar lines in (1) the discrete case, where positive rational time points\nare mapped to real-valued directed intervals through prime factorization and a freely chosen map from the\nprimes to the reals; and (2) the continuous case, where real-valued time points are associated with directed\nintervals through quantized logarithms mapped to harmonic cycles. The main result in both cases is that the\nvertical interval between voices in a tempo ratio of 𝜆𝑖 /𝜆 𝑗 is given by the homomorphism 𝜙(𝜆𝑖 /𝜆 𝑗 ), which is\na constant independent of time. Furthermore, we demonstrated that under these constructions retrograde\ninversion collapses to transposition. The musical payoff of the mathematics is the ability to compose melodic\nlines that combine in any number of voices, in any tempo ratios, either forward or in retrograde inversion\nwhile maintaining harmonic consistency—lines with an infinite number of canonic combinations.\n     Future work will proceed along a number of lines. First, there are many avenues to explore other\nmusical realizations (including a variety of harmonic cycles),8 multimedia presentations, an interactive app,\nand relation to recent work by Tymoczko [16]. Second, it is worth considering the relationships between\nthe current approach and that of Johnson, Amiot, and others in greater detail than §2.7. Third, the pitch\nsymmetry about every time point noted toward the end of §5 suggests an alternative formalization in terms\nof reflection. Fourth, it would be helpful and musically suggestive to consider the move from the discrete\nto the continuous case in greater detail: one can incorporate irrational ratios within the construction of\n§2 through Hamel bases at the expense of potentially wildly discontinuous structures, creating problems\nfor the harmonic consistency that motivated the requirement for continuity in §3.2, while the quantization\nprocess may also present difficulties for maintaining harmonic control. Relatedly, it is important to clearly\nidentify the effects of rounding errors alluded to at the end of §4. Fifth, the function 𝜙 invites discussion of\nhomomorphisms between Lewinian generalized interval systems [11] (following Tymoczko’s response [15] to\nHook [9]), particularly the GISes corresponding to products over the positive rationals and addition over the\nreals discussed in Lewin [11]. Finally, proof of the expanded aperiodicity results in §2.5 is forthcoming.\n    8 These realizations include Canonic Offerings, for string quartet, invited by Dmitri Tymoczko for a concert as part of the 2013\n\nBridges Conference: Mathematics, Music, Art, Architecture, Culture in Enschede, The Netherlands and spira mirabilis: infinite\ntempo canons, for player piano, invited by Benjamin Broening for 2018 Third Practice Music Festival at the University of Richmond,\nVA.\n\n\n                                                                18\n\fReferences\n[1] Amiot, Emmanuel, “Autosimilar Melodies,” Journal of Mathematics and Music 2, no. 3 (2008): 157–180.\n\n[2] Amiot, Emmanuel, Carlos Agon, and Moreno Andreatta, “Autosimilar Melodies and Their Implementation\n    in OpenMusic,” Proceedings of the 4th Sound and Music Computing Conference, Lefkada, Greece, 2007.\n\n[3] Archibald, R. C., “Notes on the Logarithmic Spiral, Golden Section and the Fibonacci Series,” Note V in\n    Jay Hambidge, Dynamic Symmetry: The Greek Vase, 146–157. New Haven: Yale University Press, 1920.\n\n[4] Callender, Clifton, “Formalized Accelerando: An Extension of Rhythmic Techniques in Nancarrow’s\n    Acceleration Canons,” Perspectives of New Music 39, no. 1 (2001): 188–210.\n\n[5] Callender,\n    √          Clifton, “Performing the Irrational: Paul Usher’s Arrangement of Nancarrow’s Study No. 33,\n     2/2,” Music Theory Online 20, no. 1 (2014).\n\n[6] Callender, Clifton, “Infinite Canons,” https://cliftoncallender.com/research/infinite_\n    canons/.\n\n[7] Gann, Kyle, The Music of Conlon Nancarrow. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1995.\n\n[8] Hendriks, Dimitri, Frits G. W. Dannenberg, Jörg Endrullis, Mark Dow, and Jan Willem Klop, “Arithmetic\n    Self-Similarity of Infinite Sequences,” arXiv:1201.3786 [math], 2012.\n\n[9] Hook, Julian, “Cross-Type Transformations and the Path Consistency Condition,” Music Theory Spectrum\n    29, no. 1 (2007): 1–40.\n\n[10] Johnson, Tom, Self-Similar Melodies. Paris: Editions 75, 1996.\n\n[11] Lewin, David, Generalized Musical Intervals and Transformations. New Haven: Yale University Press,\n    1987.\n\n[12] Nemire, Julie, “Convergence Points in Conlon Nancarrow’s Tempo Canons,” Music Theory Online 20,\n    no. 1 (2014).\n\n[13] Thomas, Margaret E., “Nancarrow’s Canons: Projections of Temporal and Formal Structures,” Perspec-\n    tives of New Music 38, no. 2 (2000): 106–133.\n\n[14] Thomas, Margaret, “Nancarrow’s Canons: Proportions and Processes,” Intégral 14/15 (2000/2001):\n    139–174.\n\n[15] Tymoczko, Dmitri, “Lewin, Intervals, and Transformations: A Comment on Hook,” Music Theory\n    Spectrum 30, no. 1 (2008): 164–168.\n\n[16] Tymoczko, Dmitri, “The American Mathematical Society Presents: The Music of Mathematics,”\n    International Congress of Mathematicians, Philadelphia, July 28, 2026 (guest lecture/performance).\n\n\n\n\n                                                    19\n\f", "numLines": 937, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 937}}}
